@c ----- Eval Command --------------------------------------------------
@section Eval Command

@table @code
@item eval EXPR
Evaluate an arbitrary elisp expression and print the result.  This is
the escape hatch for anything not covered by the other commands.
@example
bacon$ cmacsgi eval "(+ 40 2)"
42
bacon$ cmacsgi eval "(format-time-string \"%Y-%m-%d\")"
"2026-04-04"
@end example
@end table

@c ----- Other Original Commands ---------------------------------------
@section Other Commands

@table @code
@item find-file PATH
Open a file (alias for @code{open}).

@item message TEXT
Display a message in the CMacs minibuffer.
@end table

@c ----- Architecture --------------------------------------------------
@section Architecture

The @command{cmacsgi} builtin is a thin wrapper around the shared
@code{libcmacs-api} library (@pxref{CMacs API}).  The command
implementations, transport layer, and eval helpers all live in
@file{cmacs/api/}; the bacon module only contains the BaconCommand
GObject boilerplate and the dispatch table.

The primary IPC path (used by @code{M-x bacon}) is a Unix socketpair
with length-prefixed JSON messages:

@example
  CMacs (Emacs process)
       |
  cmacs-bacon-ipc  <--- socketpair fd ---> cmacs --bacon (child)
       |                                        |
  cmacs-eval-dispatch                     cmacsgi builtin
       |                                        |
  elisp eval / GI / file ops     libcmacs-api (FD transport backend)
@end example

@noindent
Wire protocol: @code{[4 bytes: uint32 BE length][length bytes: JSON]}.
Requests and responses are JSON objects with @code{id}, @code{method},
@code{params}/@code{result}/@code{error} fields.

The D-Bus fallback path (for external tools or standalone bacon) goes
through the session bus:

@example
  External tool / standalone bacon
       |
  GDBus proxy  --->  D-Bus session bus
       |                     |
       |              org.cmacs.Editor1
       |                     |
  CMacs (cmacs-eval-dispatch)
@end example

@noindent
Both paths share the same dispatch layer (@file{cmacs-eval-dispatch.c}),
so all methods behave identically regardless of transport.

Most subcommands build an elisp form on the client side and call the
@code{Eval} method.  The user never sees or writes elisp---the
@command{cmacsgi} command translates shell arguments into the
appropriate Lisp forms.

@section Environment Variables

@table @env
@item CMACS_IPC_FD
File descriptor number for the child side of the socketpair.  Set
automatically by @code{M-x bacon} before launching @command{cmacs
--bacon}.  When set, @command{cmacsgi} uses the fd transport (faster,
no bus daemon involved).

@item CMACS_DBUS_NAME
The D-Bus bus name of the running CMacs instance.  Set automatically
by @code{M-x bacon} as a fallback.  Example:
@code{org.cmacs.Editor.Pid12345}.  Used when @env{CMACS_IPC_FD} is
not available.

@item CMACS_MODULE_DIR
Directory containing @command{cmacsgi} module files.  Set by
@code{M-x bacon} so the child shell can locate loadable modules.
@end table

