@c ===================================================================
@node Crispy C Scripting
@chapter Crispy C Scripting

@cindex Crispy
@cindex C scripting

CMacs integrates the Crispy library for evaluating C code at runtime.
Scripts are compiled to shared objects, cached by content hash (SHA256),
and loaded into the Emacs process via @code{g_module_open()}.  Because
scripts run in-process, they can call back into Emacs through the
CMacs API (@pxref{CMacs API}).

@section Writing Config in C

Place an @file{init.c} in @file{~/.config/cmacs/} (or
@code{user-emacs-directory} as a fallback).  CMacs checks
@code{$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/cmacs/} first, then @file{~/.emacs.d/}, so
you can keep CMacs config separate from vanilla Emacs.  The file is
loaded after @file{init.el} via @code{crispy-run}.  The CMacs API
headers and library are automatically available---no manual
@code{CRISPY_PARAMS} needed.

@xref{CMacs API, , Example: init.c}, for a complete example.

@section Elisp Primitives

@table @code
@item crispy-eval CODE
Compile and execute inline C @var{CODE}.  Returns the exit code.
@item crispy-eval-string CODE
Execute C @var{CODE}, capturing stdout as a string.
@item crispy-run FILE &rest ARGS
Compile and execute a @file{.c} file with arguments.
@item crispy-compile FILE
Compile @var{FILE}, return the cached binary path.
@item crispy-repl-eval CODE
Evaluate in the persistent REPL.
@item crispy-repl-reset
Reset persistent REPL state.
@item crispy-cache-status
Return the crispy cache directory path.
@end table

@section Files

@table @file
@item cmacs/crispy/cmacs-crispy.c
C primitive implementation.  Registers the CMacs API direct dispatch
table so crispy scripts can call back into Emacs without IPC.
@item lisp/cmacs/cmacs-crispy.el
Elisp layer: REPL, minor mode, region/buffer evaluation.
@end table

@c ===================================================================
@node Gowl Wayland Compositor
@chapter Gowl Wayland Compositor

@cindex Gowl
@cindex Wayland compositor
@cindex window manager
@cindex client embedding

Gowl is a wlroots-based Wayland compositor embedded in CMacs.  When
launched with @option{--gowl}, Emacs becomes a tiling window manager:
the editor renders as a Wayland client inside its own compositor, and
other Wayland applications tile alongside or embed directly inside
Emacs windows.

The integration provides 47+ C primitives (DEFUNs) for full compositor
control from Elisp.

@section Starting the Compositor

@example
src/emacs --gowl
@end example

@noindent
Inside Emacs, the compositor is controlled via @code{cmacs-gowl-mode},
which is auto-enabled by @option{--gowl}.

@section Window Management

@table @code
@item cmacs-gowl-spawn-command COMMAND
Launch a Wayland client.
@item cmacs-gowl-focus-window
Interactively select and focus a client window.
@item cmacs-gowl-close-window
Interactively select and close a client window.
@item cmacs-gowl-list-windows
Display all managed windows in a help buffer.
@item cmacs-gowl-view-tag TAG
Switch to a tag (workspace) 1--9.
@item cmacs-gowl-send-to-tag TAG
Move focused client to a tag 1--9.
@item cmacs-gowl-set-layout LAYOUT
Set the tiling layout (tile, monocle, float).
@end table

@section Monitor Management

@cindex monitor
@cindex output
@cindex resolution
@cindex scale
@cindex transform
@cindex HiDPI

Gowl provides full control over connected monitors (outputs): listing,
mode changes, scale adjustments, transforms, and enable/disable.  All
operations work on both physical monitors (DRM backend) and nested
Wayland outputs.

@subsection Listing Monitors

@code{cmacs-gowl-list-monitors} displays a table of all outputs:

@example
Name    Mode          Position  Scale  Status   Transform
WL-1    1280x720@@60   0,0      1.0    enabled  normal
DP-1    2560x1440@@144  1280,0  1.0    enabled  normal
@end example

@noindent
From the shell:

@example
bacon> cmacsgi monitor list
WL-1
DP-1

bacon> cmacsgi monitor info WL-1
name=WL-1
mode=1280x720@@60
position=0,0
scale=1.000000
enabled=t
transform=0
@end example

@subsection Changing Resolution

@code{cmacs-gowl-set-resolution} prompts with the monitor's advertised
modes.  For outputs with no advertised modes (common for nested Wayland
sessions), specify a custom mode directly.

@example
;; From Elisp:
(gowl-set-monitor-mode 1920 1080 0 (gowl-find-monitor "WL-1"))

;; From the shell:
bacon> cmacsgi monitor set-mode WL-1 1920x1080
bacon> cmacsgi monitor set-mode DP-1 2560x1440@@144000
@end example

@noindent
The refresh rate is in millihertz (60000 = 60@dmn{Hz}).  Use 0 to match
any refresh rate, or omit the @code{@@RATE} suffix.

@subsection Scaling

Scale controls the output DPI@.  A scale of 2.0 doubles the pixel
density, halving the logical resolution.

@example
;; HiDPI (2x):
(gowl-set-monitor-scale 2.0 (gowl-find-monitor "WL-1"))

;; Lower density for more screen real-estate:
(gowl-set-monitor-scale 0.8 (gowl-find-monitor "WL-1"))
@end example

@example
bacon> cmacsgi monitor scale WL-1 2.0
bacon> cmacsgi monitor scale WL-1 0.8
bacon> cmacsgi monitor scale WL-1 1.0
@end example

@noindent
At scale 0.8 on a 1280x720 output the logical area grows to 1600x900;
at scale 2.0 it shrinks to 640x360.  Clients automatically resize to
fill the new logical area.

@subsection Transform (Rotation/Flip)

Transform values follow the Wayland @code{wl_output_transform} enum:

@multitable @columnfractions .15 .25 .6
@headitem Value @tab Name @tab Effect
@item 0 @tab normal @tab No rotation
@item 1 @tab 90 @tab Rotate 90@textdegree{} clockwise
@item 2 @tab 180 @tab Rotate 180@textdegree{}
@item 3 @tab 270 @tab Rotate 270@textdegree{} clockwise
@item 4 @tab flipped @tab Horizontal flip
@item 5 @tab flipped-90 @tab Flip + rotate 90@textdegree{}
@item 6 @tab flipped-180 @tab Flip + rotate 180@textdegree{}
@item 7 @tab flipped-270 @tab Flip + rotate 270@textdegree{}
@end multitable

@example
bacon> cmacsgi monitor transform WL-1 1   @r{;; rotate 90@textdegree{}}
bacon> cmacsgi monitor transform WL-1 0   @r{;; back to normal}
@end example

@subsection Enable / Disable

@example
bacon> cmacsgi monitor disable DP-2
bacon> cmacsgi monitor enable DP-2
@end example

@noindent
Or interactively: @kbd{M-x cmacs-gowl-toggle-monitor}.

@subsection Interactive Commands

@table @code
@item cmacs-gowl-list-monitors
Show a table of all outputs with mode, position, scale, and status.
@item cmacs-gowl-monitor-info
Show detailed info for a selected monitor.
@item cmacs-gowl-set-resolution
Interactively choose a monitor and set its resolution.
@item cmacs-gowl-set-scale SCALE
Set the scale factor on a selected monitor.
@item cmacs-gowl-set-transform
Interactively choose a transform for a selected monitor.
@item cmacs-gowl-toggle-monitor
Enable or disable a selected monitor.
@end table

@subsection How It Works

When a mode, scale, or transform change is committed via
@code{wlr_output_commit_state()}, gowl updates the monitor geometry by
querying @code{wlr_output_layout_get_box()}, which returns the correct
logical dimensions accounting for scale and transform.  This matches the
approach used by dwl.

The compositor then calls @code{gowl_compositor_arrange()}, which
re-tiles all clients on the affected monitor.  Fullscreen clients
(including the Emacs frame in standalone @option{--gowl} mode) are
resized to fill the full monitor area.  Normal tiling clients are
re-laid-out by the active layout function (tile, monocle, etc.).

On the Wayland backend (nested compositors),
@code{on_layout_change()} typically fires synchronously during the
output commit.  As a safety net, each setter also queries the layout
box and calls @code{arrange()} directly, ensuring correct behavior
even if the asynchronous path is delayed.

@section Client Embedding

Wayland clients can render @emph{inside} Emacs buffer windows.  The
client's scene node is reparented into Emacs's compositor scene tree
and positioned over the window body area.  The compositor renders the
client directly---no pixel copying, no GPU readback.  Input is routed
by focus-follows-mouse.

@example
M-x gowl-embed RET foot RET
@end example

@noindent
This spawns @command{foot} and embeds it in the current Emacs window.
The modeline, fringes, and header line remain visible around the
embedded client.

@subsection Embedding Commands

@table @code
@item gowl-embed COMMAND
Spawn @var{COMMAND} and embed it in the current Emacs window.
@item gowl-embed-client CLIENT
Embed an existing gowl client in the current window.
@item gowl-unembed
Release the embedded client back to normal tiling.
@end table

@subsection How It Works

@enumerate
@item
@code{gowl-embed} spawns a Wayland client and pre-registers its PID
so the compositor floats and hides it on map.
@item
A 100ms timer polls @code{gowl-list-clients} to match the PID.
@item
Once matched, the client is reparented into Emacs's scene tree
(@code{gowl-embed-into}) and positioned over the window body
(@code{gowl-position-embedded}).
@item
@code{window-configuration-change-hook} keeps the overlay in sync
when windows resize, split, or switch buffers.
@item
A 1-second health timer detects client exit (by PID comparison) and
cleans up the placeholder buffer.
@end enumerate

@subsection Lifecycle

@table @strong
@item Client exits
Health timer detects it gone from @code{gowl-list-clients} (compared
by PID), kills the placeholder buffer.
@item Buffer killed
@code{kill-buffer-hook} closes the Wayland client.
@item Window hidden
Overlay is hidden.  Switching back to the buffer re-shows it.
@end table

@subsection Keyboard Focus (ESC Escape-Hatch)

Embedded clients receive keyboard input directly from the compositor.
An ESC-based escape hatch lets you switch between the embedded client
and Emacs:

@table @strong
@item ESC (single press)
Returns keyboard focus to Emacs.  Subsequent keys are processed by
Emacs (e.g.@: Evil normal-mode commands).
@item ESC ESC (double press)
Sends a single ESC to the embedded client (useful for apps that use
ESC, like vim or bash with @code{set -o vi}).
@item i / a (Evil normal state)
Gives keyboard focus back to the embedded client.  Also works with
@kbd{I}, @kbd{A}, @kbd{o}, @kbd{O}, and @kbd{RET}.
@item Click
Clicking on the embedded area also returns keyboard focus to the
embedded client.
@end table

The escape hatch uses a 200@dmn{ms} timer to distinguish single from
double ESC@.  Pressing ESC followed by another key within the timeout
sends the second key to Emacs.

The underlying primitive @code{gowl-embed-focus} can be called from
Elisp to programmatically give keyboard focus to any embedded client.

