@section Compositor Signals

@cindex signals, compositor
@cindex focus-changed signal
@cindex client-added signal
@cindex client-removed signal

The @code{GowlCompositor} GObject emits the following signals.
Connect to them using @code{cmacs-gi-signal-connect} or the
convenience wrappers in @file{cmacs-gowl.el}.

@table @code
@item client-added
Emitted when a new client maps.  The callback receives one argument:
the @code{GowlClient} GObject.
@item client-removed
Emitted when a client unmaps.  The callback receives one argument:
the @code{GowlClient} GObject.
@item focus-changed
Emitted when keyboard focus changes.  The callback receives one
argument: the newly focused @code{GowlClient}, or @code{nil} if no
client is focused.
@end table

@section Modules

@cindex gowl modules
@cindex modules, gowl

Gowl provides a module system for extending the compositor.  Modules
are shared-object plugins that implement compositor interfaces
(layout providers, gap providers, keybind handlers, etc.).

When running with @option{--gowl}, call @code{gowl-start} before
using any module functions.  The @option{--gowl} command-line flag
pre-starts the compositor early (before Elisp loads), but
@code{gowl-start} is still needed to load bundled modules and
initialize the Elisp integration layer.

@example
(when (and (fboundp 'gowl-running-p) (gowl-running-p))
  (gowl-start)
  (gowl-enable-module "alpha")
  (gowl-set-wallpaper "~/Pictures/wallpaper.png"))
@end example

@table @code
@item gowl-start
Initialize module loading and Elisp integration.  Safe to call
when the compositor is already running (idempotent).
@item gowl-enable-module @var{name}
Find and load a module by name, activate it, and dispatch startup.
@item gowl-disable-module @var{name}
Deactivate a loaded module.
@item gowl-configure-module @var{name} @var{alist}
Configure a module with an alist of string key-value pairs.
@item gowl-list-modules
Return a list of loaded module info alists.
@end table

@subsection Alpha (Window Opacity)
@cindex alpha, window opacity
@cindex transparency, windows

The alpha module controls per-client window opacity with automatic
focus dimming.  Unfocused windows fade to a configurable opacity.

@example
(gowl-enable-module "alpha")
(gowl-set-focused-alpha 0.9)
(gowl-set-unfocused-alpha 0.9)
;; Per-client override:
(gowl-set-client-alpha client 0.5)
;; Set all clients at once:
(gowl-set-all-alpha 0.8)
;; Query current config:
(gowl-alpha-info)
  @result{} ((focused-alpha . 0.9) (unfocused-alpha . 0.9))
@end example

@subsubheading Seeing the Wallpaper Through Emacs

To make the Emacs frame transparent so the gowl wallpaper is visible
behind it, two things are needed:

@enumerate
@item
@strong{PGTK @code{alpha-background}}: Makes Emacs render its
background with transparent pixels.  Text and UI stay fully opaque.
@item
@strong{Compositor-level alpha}: Any client opacity less than 1.0
forces wlroots to skip the opaque-region optimization and composite
the wallpaper behind the frame.
@end enumerate

wlroots resets scene buffer opacity to 1.0 on every surface commit.
Gowl re-applies the stored alpha after each commit so the
transparency persists across frames.

@example
;; In your Emacs config (e.g. Doom config.el):
(when (and (fboundp 'gowl-running-p) (gowl-running-p))
  (gowl-start)
  (gowl-enable-module "alpha")
  (gowl-set-focused-alpha 0.9)
  (gowl-set-unfocused-alpha 0.9)
  (gowl-set-wallpaper "~/Pictures/wallpaper.png")
  (set-frame-parameter nil 'alpha-background 85)
  (add-to-list 'default-frame-alist '(alpha-background . 85))
  (gowl-set-all-alpha 0.99))
@end example

@table @code
@item gowl-set-client-alpha @var{client} @var{alpha}
Set opacity of a specific client (0.0--1.0).
@item gowl-set-all-alpha @var{alpha}
Set opacity of all clients.  Returns the number affected.
@item gowl-set-focused-alpha @var{alpha}
Set opacity for the focused window.
@item gowl-set-unfocused-alpha @var{alpha}
Set opacity for unfocused windows.
@item gowl-alpha-info
Return the current alpha configuration as an alist.
@end table

@subsection Vanitygaps
@cindex gaps, window

The vanitygaps module adds configurable inner and outer gaps between
tiled windows.

@example
(gowl-enable-module "vanitygaps")
(gowl-set-gaps '(("inner-gap" . "16") ("outer-gap" . "8")))
(gowl-gaps-info)
  @result{} ((inner-h . 16) (inner-v . 16) (outer-h . 8) (outer-v . 8))
@end example

@table @code
@item gowl-set-gaps @var{alist}
Configure gap sizes.  Keys: @code{inner-gap}, @code{outer-gap},
@code{inner-h}, @code{inner-v}, @code{outer-h}, @code{outer-v}.
@item gowl-gaps-info
Return the current gap values as an alist.
@end table

@subsection Screenlock
@cindex screenlock
@cindex screen lock

The screenlock module provides full-screen session locking with PAM
authentication.  @code{gowl-lock} and @code{gowl-locked-p} are
available as C primitives; use @code{gowl-configure-screenlock} to
set colors, font, PAM service, and auto-lock timeout.

@example
(gowl-enable-module "screenlock")
(gowl-configure-screenlock
  '(("pam-service" . "gowl")
    ("auto-lock-timeout" . "300")
    ("bg-color" . "#1e1e2e")))
@end example

@subsection Scratchpad
@cindex scratchpad

Named scratchpad windows that can be toggled on and off.

@example
(gowl-enable-module "scratchpad")
(gowl-scratchpad-toggle "terminal")
@end example

