@node Org-Ex Interactive Widgets
@chapter Org-Ex Interactive Widgets

@cindex Org-Ex
@cindex widget, embedding

Org-Ex embeds interactive widgets inside Org mode buffers.  It is built
as a GObject-native shared library (@file{liborgex-1.0.so}) with a thin
DEFUN bridge and an Elisp layer.

Activate with @code{#+ORGEX: t} in the file header or
@code{M-x cmacs-org-ex-mode}.

Widget types: @code{slider} (GTK), @code{web} (WebKit), @code{buffer}
(file embed), @code{elisp} / @code{crispy} / @code{bacon} (code
evaluation).

@example
#+BEGIN_WIDGET slider
:min 0
:max 100
:value 50
:bind temperature
#+END_WIDGET
@end example

@noindent
Features:

@itemize
@item
Reactive binding: @code{:bind} links widgets to @code{#+PROPERTY:} lines.
@item
Pub/sub channels: @code{:publish} / @code{:subscribe} for
widget-to-widget messaging.
@item
Export: HTML (live widgets), text (fallback descriptions).
@item
State persistence: ephemeral widget state saved to @file{.org-ex-state}
sidecar files.
@item
Extensible: register new widget types via
@code{cmacs-org-ex-register-widget-type} or by subclassing
@code{OrgExWidget} in C.
@end itemize

@noindent
See @file{doc/cmacs/org-ex/org-ex.texi} for the full Org-Ex manual
with API reference, custom widget creation guide, and export details.
See @file{doc_org/cmacs/org-ex/examples/} for working examples.

@c ===================================================================
@node MCP Server
@chapter MCP Server

@cindex MCP
@cindex Model Context Protocol
@cindex AI, agent control

CMacs embeds a Model Context Protocol (MCP) server directly inside the
Emacs process.  The server runs on the GLib event loop via
@code{McpUnixSocketServer} (from @code{mcp-glib}), exposing Emacs
buffers, windows, evaluation, and subsystem access to any MCP-capable AI
agent.

@section Architecture

An AI agent never connects to Emacs directly.  Instead, the
@command{cmacs-mcp} shim binary bridges @code{stdio} transport
(expected by MCP clients) to the Unix domain socket inside Emacs:

@example
MCP Client (AI agent)
    |  stdio (JSON-RPC)
    v
cmacs-mcp  (shim binary)
    |  Unix domain socket
    v
Emacs (McpUnixSocketServer on GLib main loop)
@end example

@noindent
The server is started automatically by @code{cmacs-mcp-mode} or
manually via @code{cmacs-mcp-start}.  The socket path defaults to
@file{$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/cmacs-mcp/@var{PID}.sock}.

@section Tools

MCP tools are grouped by category.  Each tool name is prefixed with its
category (e.g., @code{eval}, @code{buffer_list_buffers}).

@subsection eval

@table @code
@item eval
Evaluate an Elisp expression and return the result.
@item describe_function
Return the docstring and arglist of a function.
@item describe_variable
Return the docstring and value of a variable.
@item apropos
Search for symbols matching a pattern.
@item completions
Return completion candidates for a prefix.
@end table

@subsection buffer

@table @code
@item buffer_list_buffers
List all buffers with metadata (size, mode, file, modified).
@item buffer_get_buffer_content
Return buffer text, optionally by line range.
@item buffer_set_buffer_content
Replace buffer content or a region.
@item buffer_create_buffer
Create a new empty buffer.
@item buffer_kill_buffer
Kill a buffer by name.
@item buffer_switch_to_buffer
Switch the current window to a buffer.
@item buffer_save_buffer
Save a buffer to its visited file.
@item buffer_find_file
Open a file and return its buffer name.
@end table

@subsection window

@table @code
@item window_list_windows
List all windows across all frames.
@item window_list_frames
List all frames with geometry and display info.
@item window_split_window
Split a window horizontally or vertically.
@item window_delete_window
Delete a window.
@item window_select_window
Select a window by ID.
@end table

@subsection input

@table @code
@item input_send_keys
Inject a key sequence as if typed by the user.
@item input_execute_command
Execute an interactive command by name.
@end table

@subsection process

@table @code
@item process_list_processes
List all subprocesses with status and command.
@item process_send_to_process
Send a string to a running process.
@end table

@subsection debug

@table @code
@item debug_backtrace
Return the current Lisp backtrace.
@item debug_memory_info
Return GC and memory statistics.
@item debug_process_status
Return Emacs process resource usage.
@item debug_recent_messages
Return the last N lines from @code{*Messages*}.
@item debug_describe_mode
Describe active major and minor modes.
@item debug_list_hooks
List hooks and their attached functions.
@item debug_profiler_start
Start the Emacs profiler (CPU, memory, or both).
@item debug_profiler_stop
Stop the profiler.
@item debug_profiler_report
Return the profiler report as text.
@end table

@subsection gi (conditional)

These tools are registered only when CMacs is built with
@option{--with-cmacs-gi}.

@table @code
@item gi_gi_call
Call a GObject Introspection function by namespace, name, and args.
@item gi_gi_list_namespaces
List all available GI namespaces.
@item gi_gi_describe
Describe a GI type, function, or signal.
@item gi_gi_list_functions
List functions in a GI namespace.
@end table

@subsection gowl (conditional)

These tools are registered only when CMacs is built with
@option{--with-cmacs-gowl}.

@table @code
@item gowl_gowl_list_clients
List Wayland clients managed by Gowl.
@item gowl_gowl_focused_client
Return the currently focused client.
@item gowl_gowl_spawn
Spawn a Wayland client process.
@item gowl_gowl_list_monitors
List connected monitors and their geometry.
@end table

@section Resources

MCP resources expose Emacs state as URI-addressable objects that
clients can read or subscribe to:

@table @code
@item buffer://@var{name}
Contents of the named buffer.
@item file://@var{path}
Contents of a file on disk.
@item messages://
The @code{*Messages*} buffer (subscribable for live updates).
@item variable://@var{name}
The value of an Elisp variable.
@end table

@section Prompts

The server registers reusable prompt templates:

@table @code
@item debug_session
A structured prompt for debugging a problem in the current buffer,
gathering backtrace, mode info, and recent messages automatically.
@item code_review
A prompt for reviewing code in a buffer, collecting content and
mode context for the AI agent.
@end table

@section The cmacs-mcp Shim Binary

The @command{cmacs-mcp} binary (@file{tools/cmacs-mcp/}) bridges MCP
@code{stdio} transport to the Emacs Unix socket.  Socket discovery
follows this order:

@enumerate
@item
@option{--socket @var{PATH}} command-line argument.
@item
@option{--name @var{NAME}} looks up
@file{$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/cmacs-mcp/@var{NAME}.sock}.
@item
@env{CMACS_MCP_SOCKET} environment variable.
@item
Auto-discover: scan @file{$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/cmacs-mcp/} for a
single @file{.sock} file.
@end enumerate

@section Elisp Primitives

@table @code
@item cmacs-mcp-server-p
Return @code{t} if the MCP server is currently running.
@item cmacs-mcp-socket-path
Return the active socket path, or @code{nil}.
@item cmacs-mcp-session-count
Return the number of connected MCP sessions.
@item cmacs-mcp-start
Start the MCP server (creates socket, begins accepting connections).
@item cmacs-mcp-stop
Stop the MCP server (closes all sessions, removes socket).
@end table

@section Configure

Enable with @option{--with-cmacs-mcp} (auto-detected when
@code{mcp-glib-1.0} is available via @code{pkg-config}).

@section File Reference

@table @file
@item cmacs/mcp/cmacs-mcp.c
Server lifecycle, socket management, session handling.
@item cmacs/mcp/cmacs-mcp.h
Public header for the MCP subsystem.
@item cmacs/mcp/cmacs-mcp-tools.c
Tool registration and dispatch (all categories).
@item cmacs/mcp/cmacs-mcp-resources.c
Resource and prompt registration.
@item tools/cmacs-mcp/
Source for the @command{cmacs-mcp} shim binary.
@item lisp/cmacs/cmacs-mcp.el
Elisp layer: @code{cmacs-mcp-mode}, configuration, hooks.
@end table

@c ===================================================================
@node Build Configuration
@chapter Build Configuration

@section Configure Flags

CMacs features are auto-detected at configure time.  For a
fully-featured build matching Fedora defaults, use:

@example
./configure \
  --with-pgtk \
  --with-cairo \
  --with-dbus \
  --with-harfbuzz \
  --with-modules \
  --with-native-compilation=aot \
  --with-tree-sitter \
  --with-sqlite3 \
  --with-json \
  --with-rsvg \
  --with-jpeg \
  --with-png \
  --with-gif \
  --with-tiff \
  --with-webp \
  --with-xpm \
  --with-gpm=no \
  --with-cmacs-glib \
  --with-cmacs-gi \
  --with-cmacs-crispy \
  --with-cmacs-bacon \
  --with-cmacs-gowl \
  --with-cmacs-podomation \
  --with-cmacs-org-ex
@end example

@noindent
To install somewhere other than @file{/usr/local}, pass
@option{--prefix} at configure time.  For example, to install under
@file{~/.local}:

@example
./configure --prefix=$HOME/.local \
  ...                              # same flags as above
make -j$(nproc)
make install                       # no sudo needed for ~/.local
@end example

@noindent
@code{--with-cmacs-bacon} builds libbacon statically from
@file{deps/bacon/} and links it into the CMacs binary.  For distro
packaging, use @code{--with-cmacs-bacon=system} to link against a
system-installed @code{bacon-1.0} via pkg-config instead.

@section Dependencies

@multitable @columnfractions .25 .35 .4
